item.text:
**Reused Evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0002"],"lenses":["5B.2"]}
Jokowi’s constitutional executive authority matters for attribution here because 2022 fiscal, tax, and cabinet choices were made through the presidency and ministerial appointments.

**New Evidence**
The evidence below centers on 2022 fiscal management, tax reform, subsidy reallocation, inflation response, growth outcomes, distribution, and the remaining corruption/capture constraints.

- World Bank tax system loan
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"New Support Will Help Strengthen Indonesia’s Tax System, Improve Development Spending","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06-24","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/06/24/new-support-will-help-strengthen-indonesia-tax-system-improve-development-spending","claim":"The World Bank approved a $750 million loan to help Indonesia increase tax revenue, improve tax system equity, and strengthen institutions for more efficient planning and spending.","locator":"lines 143-151","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official World Bank press release with dated, specific policy measures and financing terms.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 fiscal-policy priorities and implementation support.","ruler_attribution":"High for national fiscal policy direction under Jokowi; implementation also depended on the finance ministry and legislative/legal follow-through.","contrary_evidence":["This is financing support, not proof that all reforms worked fully in 2022.","The press release also notes low revenue intake and shallow financial markets, which limited the baseline."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.4","5B.6","5B.7"]}

- Harmonized Taxation Law
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Gov’t to Optimize Fair State Revenue through Harmonized Taxation Law, Minister Says","publisher":"Sekretariat Kabinet Republik Indonesia","publication_date":"2021-10-08","url":"https://setkab.go.id/en/govt-to-optimize-fair-state-revenue-through-harmonized-taxation-law-minister-says/","claim":"Sri Mulyani said the Harmonized Taxation Law was a milestone of Indonesia’s tax reforms, aiming to improve growth, optimize state revenue, realize fair taxation, provide legal certainty, reform administration, broaden the tax base, and introduce 2022 VAT and income-tax changes.","locator":"lines 37-43","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official cabinet-secretariat transcription of the finance minister’s policy explanation with concrete tax measures and implementation dates.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Pre-2022 enactment that directly shaped 2022 tax policy and implementation.","ruler_attribution":"High for policy direction because the law was government policy under Jokowi’s administration, though ministerial leadership was central.","contrary_evidence":["The item states intent and design, not final economic outcomes.","It reflects ministerial explanation rather than an independent audit of implementation quality."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.4","5B.6","5B.7"]}

- Voluntary tax disclosure
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Menkeu Ajak Wajib Pajak Manfaatkan Program Pengungkapan Sukarela","publisher":"Sekretariat Kabinet Republik Indonesia","publication_date":"2022-02-07","url":"https://setkab.go.id/menkeu-ajak-wajib-pajak-manfaatkan-program-pengungkapan-sukarela/","claim":"The finance minister said the 2022 voluntary disclosure program gave taxpayers until June 30, 2022 to disclose unmet obligations voluntarily and improve compliance, with special treatment for previously undeclared assets and a small revenue yield by early February.","locator":"lines 46-57","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government announcement with specific dates, rates, and early participation figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers a 2022 tax-compliance initiative.","ruler_attribution":"Medium-high for tax administration under Jokowi; the initiative is minister-led but part of the national economic policy line.","contrary_evidence":["A voluntary program does not prove broad fairness or full compliance.","Early participation and receipts are positive but limited evidence of long-run structural improvement."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.5","5B.7"]}

- Fuel-subsidy reallocation
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Penyataan Pers Presiden RI mengenai Pengalihan Subsidi BBM","publisher":"Sekretariat Kabinet Republik Indonesia","publication_date":"2022-09-03","url":"https://setkab.go.id/penyataan-pers-presiden-ri-mengenai-pengalihan-subsidi-bbm/","claim":"Jokowi said the BBM subsidy budget had tripled to Rp502.4 trillion, that more than 70 percent benefited wealthier car owners, and that the government would shift part of the subsidy into targeted cash transfers and wage support for poorer households and workers.","locator":"lines 52-58","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential transcript with concrete fiscal numbers, targeting logic, and named transfer programs.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers a major 2022 policy choice made in response to the fuel-price shock.","ruler_attribution":"High; this was a presidential decision and public justification, though execution was shared with ministries and local governments.","contrary_evidence":["This is self-justifying executive rhetoric, though the quoted figures are concrete and testable.","The policy was a response to a shock, not proof of broader structural prosperity."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.9"]}

- Local social protection rule
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"134/PMK.07/2022 - Belanja Wajib dalam rangka Penanganan Dampak Inflasi Tahun Anggaran 2022","publisher":"JDIH Kementerian Keuangan","publication_date":"2022-09-05","url":"https://jdih.kemenkeu.go.id/dok/134-pmk-07-2022/summary","claim":"PMK 134/PMK.07/2022 required local governments to budget social-protection spending for October-December 2022, including support for ojek, UMKM, fishers, and transport subsidies, funded at least at 2 percent of the general transfer base.","locator":"summary bullets on social-protection budgeting and permissible uses","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official legal summary with concrete spending rules and implementation requirements.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers a 2022 inflation-response spending rule.","ruler_attribution":"High for national fiscal design; the rule was issued by the finance ministry under Jokowi’s administration, though subnational execution varied.","contrary_evidence":["This sets a rule; it does not by itself prove local compliance or household outcomes.","A mandated floor may still be too blunt or unevenly targeted in practice."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.9"]}

- IMF Article IV 2022
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2022 Article IV Consultation with Indonesia","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-03-22","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/03/22/pr2284-imf-executive-board-concludes-2022-article-iv-consultation-with-indonesia","claim":"The IMF said Indonesia’s authorities had responded with a bold, comprehensive, well-coordinated policy package that maintained economic and financial stability, and it welcomed gradual withdrawal of extraordinary support, a return to the fiscal rule, and using subsidy savings to strengthen the social safety net.","locator":"lines 240-249","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IMF surveillance summary with explicit policy assessment and recommendations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers Indonesia’s 2022 macro-policy environment and the inherited recovery phase.","ruler_attribution":"High for fiscal and macro-policy choices under Jokowi, but not exclusive credit because BI, accumulated buffers, and global conditions mattered.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF also says the outlook remained tilted to the downside and recovery benefited from accumulated policy buffers.","Some positive outcomes were not uniquely Jokowi’s; they also reflected pre-existing buffers and external conditions."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- BI inflation response
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Monetary Policy Report - Quarter IV 2022","publisher":"Bank Indonesia","publication_date":"2023-01-25","url":"https://www.bi.go.id/en/publikasi/laporan/Pages/Laporan-Kebijakan-Moneter-Triwulan-IV-2022.aspx","claim":"Bank Indonesia reported that 2022 CPI inflation ended at 5.51 percent after subsidized fuel prices were adjusted in September 2022, and said lower inflation reflected close coordination between the government and BI through a front-loaded, pre-emptive response and food-inflation control measures.","locator":"lines 205-207 and 187-200","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official central-bank report with specific inflation figures and policy attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly reports end-2022 inflation outcomes and the policy response taken during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"High for the fuel-price decision and fiscal coordination; medium for inflation outcomes because BI policy was independent and global price pressures were material.","contrary_evidence":["Inflation still exceeded the target band, so the stabilization was partial rather than complete.","The report credits both government and Bank Indonesia, not Jokowi alone."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- World Bank December 2022 rebound
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Indonesia’s Economy Sees Rebound in 2022 following Post-Covid Reopening","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-15","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/15/indonesia-s-economy-sees-rebound-in-2022-following-post-covid-reopening","claim":"The World Bank projected 5.2 percent growth in 2022, said Indonesia’s conditions remained stable amid global shocks, and noted that growth was boosted by reopening and commodity prices while fiscal consolidation was helped by high commodity revenues and lower COVID-related spending.","locator":"lines 173-177","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official World Bank press release with concrete macroeconomic assessment and explicit attribution caveats.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2022 economy and near-end-2022 fiscal conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the outcome reflects Jokowi-era policy, but the source explicitly credits reopening and commodity prices as major drivers.","contrary_evidence":["The same source attributes much of the rebound to reopening and commodity prices, limiting direct ruler credit.","It also says downside risks remained significant."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.8","5B.10"]}

- Tax revenue outperformance
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Tax Revenue Reaches 110.06 Percent","publisher":"Sekretariat Kabinet Republik Indonesia","publication_date":"2022-12-20","url":"https://setkab.go.id/en/tax-revenue-reaches-110-06-percent/","claim":"By 14 December 2022, Indonesia’s tax revenue had reached 110.06 percent of target and grown 41.93 percent year on year, with the finance minister attributing the result to good economic growth, recovery, higher commodity prices, and tax-law reform.","locator":"opening paragraphs of the press item","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government press item with a concrete year-end fiscal result and attribution statement, though it is a ministerial readout rather than an audit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers year-end 2022 fiscal performance.","ruler_attribution":"Medium-high for fiscal-policy success; the result is partly policy-driven but also partly windfall-driven.","contrary_evidence":["The stated drivers include commodity prices and broad recovery, so the result is not purely a Jokowi-policy effect.","A press item is not a full independent audit of revenue quality or sustainability."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.10"]}

- 2022 GDP growth
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ekonomi Indonesia Tahun 2022 Tumbuh 5,31 Persen","publisher":"Badan Pusat Statistik Indonesia","publication_date":"2023-02-06","url":"https://www.bps.go.id/id/pressrelease/2023/02/06/1997/indonesia-gdp-growth-rate-5-31-percent--2022-.html","claim":"BPS reported that Indonesia’s 2022 economy grew 5.31 percent, with GDP per capita of Rp71.0 million or US$4,783.9, and that transportation/storage and exports were the leading growth components.","locator":"abstract bullets 1-4","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official national statistics release with clear 2022 macro indicators.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly reports full-year 2022 economic outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"Low-to-moderate because this is an aggregate national outcome affected by reopening, commodities, and external demand as well as policy.","contrary_evidence":["The statistic itself does not isolate Jokowi’s contribution.","The growth mix was heavily influenced by sectors and external conditions beyond the presidency."],"lenses":["5B.10"]}

- Employment and wages
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Agustus 2022: Tingkat Pengangguran Terbuka (TPT) sebesar 5,86 persen dan Rata-rata upah buruh sebesar 3,07 juta rupiah per bulan","publisher":"Badan Pusat Statistik Indonesia","publication_date":"2022-11-07","url":"https://www.bps.go.id/id/pressrelease/2022/11/07/1916/agustus-2022--tingkat-pengangguran-terbuka--tpt--sebesar-5-86-persen-dan-rata-rata-upah-buruh-sebesar-3-07-juta-rupiah-per-bulan.html","claim":"BPS reported that the August 2022 open unemployment rate was 5.86 percent, down 0.63 points from August 2021, while average labor wages were Rp3.07 million per month and employment increased by 4.25 million people year on year.","locator":"abstract bullets 2-7","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official labor-force survey release with direct 2022 labor-market figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers a 2022 labor-market snapshot.","ruler_attribution":"Low-to-moderate because this is an economy-wide outcome shaped by recovery, not a ruler-only decision.","contrary_evidence":["The release is a snapshot, not a causal policy audit.","It still shows underemployment and pandemic scarring, so household security was improving but not solved."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- Poverty in September 2022
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Persentase Penduduk Miskin September 2022 naik menjadi 9,57 persen","publisher":"Badan Pusat Statistik Indonesia","publication_date":"2023-01-16","url":"https://www.bps.go.id/id/pressrelease/2023/01/16/2015/persentase-pendekujitk-miskin-septur-2022-naik--adi-9-57-persen.html","claim":"BPS reported that the poverty rate in September 2022 was 9.57 percent, or 26.36 million people, slightly higher than March 2022 but lower than September 2021.","locator":"abstract bullets 1-4","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official statistics release with direct poverty data and comparison points.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly reports a target-year household welfare outcome as of September 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Low-to-moderate because poverty is an aggregate outcome influenced by many factors, though policy responses to inflation and transfers matter.","contrary_evidence":["Poverty rose slightly from March 2022, so the household picture was not uniformly improving.","The measure reflects broad conditions, not Jokowi’s actions alone."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}

- Inequality in September 2022
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Gini Ratio September 2022 tercatat sebesar 0,381","publisher":"Badan Pusat Statistik Indonesia","publication_date":"2023-01-16","url":"https://www.bps.go.id/id/pressrelease/2023/01/16/2034/gini-ratio-septem-ber-2022-tercatat-sebesar-0-381.html","claim":"BPS reported that Indonesia’s September 2022 Gini ratio was 0.381, down from 0.384 in March 2022 and unchanged from September 2021.","locator":"abstract bullets 1-4","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official inequality statistics release with explicit comparison points.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly reports a target-year distributional outcome.","ruler_attribution":"Low-to-moderate because inequality is shaped by many structural factors, though transfer policy and inflation response can affect it.","contrary_evidence":["The change was small, so the distributional improvement was modest.","The measure does not isolate any single policy, leader, or region."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}

- Corruption perception score
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Corruption Perceptions Index 2022","publisher":"Transparency International","publication_date":"2023-01-31","url":"https://www.transparency.org/en/cpi/2022/index/idn","claim":"Transparency International reported Indonesia’s 2022 CPI score at 34 out of 100, rank 110 of 180, down 4 points from 2021, indicating continued weak anti-corruption performance.","locator":"country page and global-results text for Indonesia","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Well-known cross-country corruption index with a clear 2022 country score, though perception-based rather than direct observation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2022 corruption environment.","ruler_attribution":"Medium: the score reflects the broader governing environment under Jokowi, but it is not a ruler-specific audit and is perception-based.","contrary_evidence":["The CPI is a perception index, not a direct measure of a single leader’s actions.","It does not distinguish inherited problems from Jokowi-era choices."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.7"]}

- Cabinet reshuffle, official record
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Jokowi Reshuffles Indonesia Onward Cabinet","publisher":"Sekretariat Kabinet Republik Indonesia","publication_date":"2022-06-15","url":"https://setkab.go.id/en/president-jokowi-reshuffles-indonesia-onward-cabinet/","claim":"Jokowi inaugurated two ministers and three vice ministers on June 15, 2022 for the remainder of the 2019-2024 term, under presidential decrees dismissing and appointing ministers and vice ministers.","locator":"lines 37-44","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official cabinet-secretariat record of actual ministerial appointments and presidential decrees.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly covers a 2022 cabinet appointment event.","ruler_attribution":"High for appointment authority and cabinet composition; it does not itself show whether the appointees were economically optimal.","contrary_evidence":["This proves appointment power, not economic competence.","Two of the new appointees were not clearly economic technocrats, so the item is only partial evidence for 5B.2."],"lenses":["5B.2"]}

- Mixed cabinet-reshuffle interpretation
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Jokowi orders seventh Cabinet reshuffle","publisher":"The Jakarta Post","publication_date":"2022-06-15","url":"https://www.thejakartapost.com/indonesia/2022/06/15/jokowi-orders-seventh-cabinet-reshuffle.html","claim":"The Jakarta Post reported that the trade minister was replaced after soaring cooking-oil prices and nationwide shortages, and that Zulkifli Hasan’s appointment continued a revolving-door pattern in the trade portfolio.","locator":"paragraphs 3-4 of the article","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reputable newspaper reporting on a concrete cabinet change, but with some interpretive framing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the June 2022 cabinet reshuffle during the target year.","ruler_attribution":"High for Jokowi’s choice of appointees and response to the cooking-oil crisis; the underlying price shock was broader than the presidency.","contrary_evidence":["The article is journalistic interpretation rather than a formal economic audit.","The reshuffle can be read as crisis response rather than proof of long-term technocratic selection."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.5","5B.7"]}

**Search And Rejection Summary**
I searched roughly 30 documents across IMF, World Bank, Bank Indonesia, BPS, Kemenkeu, Setkab, Transparency International, Reuters/Jakarta Post, and a few regional/investigative outlets.

Kept:
- IMF 2022 Article IV consultation press release and country-report snippet.
- World Bank June 2022 tax-system loan press release.
- World Bank December 2022 Indonesia Economic Prospects press release.
- Bank Indonesia Q4 2022 Monetary Policy Report.
- Setkab tax-law and subsidy transcript pages.
- Kemenkeu legal summary for PMK 134/2022.
- BPS 2022 GDP, labor, poverty, and inequality releases.
- Transparency International CPI 2022 page.
- Setkab’s official cabinet-reshuffle record.
- Jakarta Post’s cabinet-reshuffle report.

Rejected or downgraded:
- Repeated World Bank IEP event pages and slides that mostly duplicated the December 2022 macro narrative.
- Several Kemenkeu pages that failed to open cleanly in-browser or were duplicated across APBN briefings.
- Reuters Connect image-only results.
- Broader trade/investment pages that did not add ruler-specific 2022 evidence.
- Older background items from 2021-2012 that were only useful if they directly explained 2022 implementation; most were not needed once the 2021 tax-law and 2022 implementation pieces were captured.
- 2024 retrospective fuel-subsidy material was not necessary because 2022 primary material already covered the same policy shift.

**Remaining Gaps**
- `5B.2` is still the weakest lens. I have evidence that Jokowi reshuffled the cabinet and that the reshuffle had political-crisis and coalition elements, but I do not have a clean 2022 source showing he systematically appointed clearly superior economic professionals over loyalists.
- `5B.4` and `5B.5` would benefit from more direct firm-level or competition-policy evidence, especially on monopolies, connected business privileges, procurement discipline, and market entry.
- `5B.8` remains only partly covered by headline poverty, inequality, and labor-market data. More direct household-income or regional-distribution evidence would help.
- Attribution is mixed on the upside: growth, tax receipts, and stability were helped materially by reopening, commodity prices, and inherited buffers, so the positive macro evidence does not translate into full ruler credit by itself.
- The corruption picture is still largely indirect: CPI and cabinet-crisis reporting point to weak anti-corruption performance, but a stronger 2022 ruler-specific anti-capture audit would help separate inherited corruption from Jokowi-era choices.
